Reused evidence: none.

New evidence:
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Voices Against Indifference","publisher":"ARTICLE 19","publication_date":"2023-03-28","url":"https://www.article19.org/resources/mexico-voices-against-indifference/","claim":"In 2022 Mexico recorded 696 attacks against the press, 12 journalists were murdered, 42.53% of documented attacks were perpetrated directly by state actors, and the executive branch made 176 stigmatizing comments against media, journalists, and civil society.","locator":"lines 69-77","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist annual report with quantified 2022 documentation and clear methodology.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 coverage published in March 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: captures AMLO-era executive behavior and state-actor conduct in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["Not every attack was personally ordered by the president.","Some violence was also driven by criminal actors rather than the federal executive."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.3","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"In 2022, journalist killings continue unabated in Mexico amid a climate of impunity","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/08/in-2022-journalist-killings-continue-unabated-in-mexico-amid-a-climate-of-impunity/","claim":"At least 13 journalists were killed in Mexico in the first eight months of 2022, the highest number CPJ had ever documented in the country in a single year; CPJ also noted that at least nine reporters and dozens of human rights defenders had been killed while technically under federal protection.","locator":"lines 515-522 and 535","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist press-freedom monitoring with direct 2022 counts and protection-mechanism commentary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but substantial: reflects the AMLO administration's security and protection failure environment.","contrary_evidence":["Motive is unclear in some killings.","Some cases may have been cartel-driven rather than directly state-ordered."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The right to protest is under threat in Mexico","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/05/right-to-protest-under-threat-mexico/","claim":"Police repressed 93 people, mostly female students from Mactumactzá; Amnesty reported excessive force, racist/classist/misogynistic insults, sexual-violence threats, and psychological torture during detention.","locator":"lines 65-69 and following paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous human-rights reporting with detailed victim accounts.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Adjacent to the target year; it documents the protest climate shaping 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the abuse was by state police under AMLO-era governance, not a direct federal order evidenced here.","contrary_evidence":["The incident was handled by state police rather than the federal executive.","It is one incident, not by itself a nationwide pattern."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Asylum Seekers Face Abuses at Southern Border","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-06-06","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/06/mexico-asylum-seekers-face-abuses-southern-border","claim":"HRW reported that President López Obrador intensified immigration restrictions and enforcement, including new visa requirements and domestic-status checks; soldiers and immigration agents ran checkpoints, and the Mexican Supreme Court had ruled those checkpoints unconstitutional.","locator":"lines 645-648","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative human-rights report based on interviews, documents, and corroborating evidence.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence based on interviews extending into May 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: these were executive policy choices and enforcement practices under AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["Some officials framed the policy as migration control rather than coercion.","The Supreme Court ruling provides a legal check, not proof of executive compliance."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Militarization of public security will lead to more human rights violations and perpetuate impunity","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-09","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/09/mexico-militarizing-public-security-human-rights-violations-impunity/","claim":"Amnesty said that on 31 August 2022 President López Obrador proposed reforms to place the National Guard under the military control of Sedena, allow active military personnel to serve without leaving their military posts, send alleged human-rights crimes to military courts, and require military training for all National Guard personnel.","locator":"lines 75-76","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous advocacy response quoting the executive proposal and its key provisions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year policy evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO attribution: this was his proposal.","contrary_evidence":["Supporters argued the change would improve security coordination.","The proposal itself does not prove every downstream abuse, but it does show a militarizing choice."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.5","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Obstruction Threatens Probe into Disappeared Mexico Students","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-19","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/19/obstruction-threatens-probe-disappeared-mexico-students","claim":"HRW reported that the special prosecutor resigned in September 2022, the attorney general had been obstructing the Ayotzinapa investigation by refusing warrants and forensic work and by intervening to cancel warrants against military and government officials, and that a presidential ally with no human-rights investigation experience was appointed to replace him.","locator":"lines 620-624","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed contemporaneous analysis with specific institutional actions and named actors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but substantial: the case involved presidential orders, military resistance, and the AMLO administration's handling of accountability.","contrary_evidence":["HRW also notes that August 2022 saw 83 arrest warrants, showing real earlier progress.","This is a case-specific accountability failure, not proof of generalized nationwide terror."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.9","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La ONU-DH expresa su preocupación por actos de vigilancia ilícita contra personas defensoras de derechos humanos y periodistas","publisher":"Naciones Unidas en México","publication_date":"2022-10-11","url":"https://mexico.un.org/es/202890-la-onu-dh-expresa-su-preocupaci%C3%B3n-por-actos-de-vigilancia-il%C3%ADcita-contra-personas-defensoras","claim":"UN human-rights staff said leaked Sedena documents or a government-acquired software package revealed illicit surveillance against journalists and human-rights defenders, calling it grave, intimidating, and contrary to a safe environment for criticism.","locator":"lines 59-67","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN country-office statement with explicit concern about government-linked surveillance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: it implicates federal authorities and the armed forces under AMLO.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is not itself a court finding of personal presidential orders.","It addresses surveillance/intimidation more than kinetic violence."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.3","3B.5","3B.8"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rinde informe presidencia de la Comisión para la Verdad y Acceso a la Justicia en el caso Ayotzinapa","publisher":"Presidencia de la República","publication_date":"2022-08-18","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/rinde-informe-presidencia-de-la-comision-para-la-verdad-y-acceso-a-la-justicia-en-el-caso-ayotzinapa","claim":"The presidency said AMLO's commitment was to tell the truth, that 41,168 documents had been reviewed, the report and annexes were public, three students had been identified, and there were sufficient elements to continue investigating 33 people.","locator":"lines 67-76","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official presidential statement with concrete accountability steps and document totals.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Direct AMLO-cabinet accountability and transparency effort, though it concerns a historical atrocity rather than a current incident.","contrary_evidence":["This is about a 2014 crime, not a broad fix for 2022 violence.","Later obstruction allegations complicate the picture."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico ratifies ILO Convention on Violence and Harassment","publisher":"International Labour Organization","publication_date":"2022-07-06","url":"https://www.ilo.org/resource/news/mexico-ratifies-ilo-convention-violence-and-harassment","claim":"Mexico deposited ratification of ILO Convention No. 190, the first international treaty specifically addressing violence and harassment in the world of work.","locator":"ILO news release / NORMLEX ratification record","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ILO ratification announcement and treaty-status record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year institutional action.","ruler_attribution":"State-level, linked to the AMLO administration through cabinet and senate action rather than a single presidential order.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification is not the same as implementation.","It is strongest as a prevention/standards signal, not as proof of immediate safety improvement."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bachelet llama a México a redoblar esfuerzos tras alcanzar la trágica cifra de más de 100,000 desapariciones","publisher":"ONU-DH","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://hchr.org.mx/comunicados/bachelet-llama-a-mexico-a-redoblar-esfuerzos-tras-alcanzar-la-tragica-cifra-de-mas-de-100000-desapariciones/","claim":"Michelle Bachelet said Mexico had officially surpassed 100,000 disappearances and called for more efforts to guarantee truth and justice; the UN note adds that more than 97% of date-known disappearances occurred after December 2006 in the militarized-security era.","locator":"lines 73-75","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN human-rights statement with an exact figure and contextual breakdown.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year context and inherited baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect baseline rather than AMLO-specific causation; it frames the inherited safety crisis he governed.","contrary_evidence":["Much of the disappearance crisis predates AMLO.","The source is baseline context, not a ruling on his personal responsibility."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico - MEX 14/2022","publisher":"Special Procedures of the Human Rights Council","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://spcommreports.ohchr.org/TmSearch/RelCom?code=MEX+14%2F2022","claim":"The UN communication concerns the killings of Esmeralda Gallardo Sánchez and María del Carmen Vázquez, two women searchers, in a context of growing attacks against relatives and collectives searching for disappeared people in Mexico.","locator":"database summary row for MEX 14/2022","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN communications database, but the item is an allegation/concern summary rather than a merits finding.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant: it highlights failures of state protection for search collectives under the AMLO administration.","contrary_evidence":["The communication records alleged information, not adjudicated findings.","It speaks to a protection gap rather than direct presidential order."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"EL PROCEDIMIENTO DE REVISIÓN MIGRATORIA QUE SE EFECTÚA EN LUGARES DISTINTOS AL DE TRÁNSITO INTERNACIONAL ES INCONSTITUCIONAL POR APLICARSE A PERSONAS NACIONALES Y EXTRANJERAS SIN DISTINCIÓN ALGUNA: PRIMERA SALA","publisher":"Suprema Corte de Justicia de la Nación","publication_date":"2022-05-19","url":"https://www.internet2.scjn.gob.mx/red2/comunicados/noticia.asp?id=6906","claim":"The First Chamber held that migration review procedures conducted away from international transit points were unconstitutional because they violated free movement, equality, and non-discrimination and disproportionately affected Indigenous and Afro-Mexican communities.","locator":"Comunicado No. 184/2022","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Supreme Court press release summarizing a specific 2022 ruling.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year judicial remedy.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect state-level check on executive migration policy; the remedy was judicial, not presidential.","contrary_evidence":["This is a court check, not proof of executive restraint by itself.","Implementation would still matter after the ruling."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.7","3B.8","3B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 30 documents across ARTICLE 19, CPJ, Amnesty, HRW, UN/OHCHR, SCJN, the Mexican presidency, and ILO records, plus several local-language and press items.
- I retained 12 distinct URLs because they covered the main 3B themes without excessive duplication.
- I rejected duplicate protest writeups and repeated journalist-killing coverage when the stronger specialist source already captured the same fact pattern.
- I rejected several 2021-only protest and repression pieces unless they were directly useful as near-baseline context for the 2022 climate.
- I rejected broad Mexico human-rights overviews when a narrower 2022 document offered the same claim more precisely.
- I rejected later 2023-2025 retrospectives unless they were needed to confirm a 2022 fact or explain unresolved accountability; most were not.
- I rejected items focused mainly on economics, foreign policy, or international conduct because they belong to other chapters.
- I rejected pieces that were too generic, too duplicative of local priors, or too weakly tied to AMLO-level attribution.
- One or two official or treaty pages were blocked or difficult to open directly, so I relied on the search snippet or a parallel official record where appropriate.

Remaining gaps:
- Direct evidence of who ordered specific police, military, or intelligence abuses in 2022 remains limited; most evidence is state-level pattern evidence rather than smoking-gun command documents.
- The strongest negative record is around journalists, protest policing, migrants, surveillance, and Ayotzinapa accountability, but there is still no single source that ties every abuse to a presidential directive.
- More searching could still help on prison abuse, local prosecutor accountability, and specific chain-of-command responsibility for particular killings or disappearances.
- The current record is already enough to show a recurring pattern of state-linked abuse and weak remedy, alongside some real but limited corrective steps on transparency, treaty ratification, and judicial checks.
